lucy_cs: FYI client 'Bluefield' onboarding noticed a weird mix of `insufficient_quota` and 429s during a Node SDK ramp. They reported ~180 concurrent sessions. Can infra take a look?

ryan_oncall: Can you paste the few log lines + their init pattern? Also, did they run preflight on every user tab?

lucy_cs: Logs: 
```
[2026-04-05T10:02:11Z] uid=bf-01 auth=ok token_age=2s status=200 request_id=xyz
[2026-04-05T10:02:11Z] uid=bf-02 auth=failed error=insufficient_quota
[2026-04-05T10:02:11Z] uid=bf-03 status=429 msg="rate limit exceeded"
[2026-04-05T10:02:11Z] uid=bf-04 status=200 auth=ok token_age=1s
```
Init: 
```
const rw = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
// called on each new session / tab
await rw.preflight();
```

tom_infra: This is classic preflight fanout. Onboarding guardrails will flip to protect shared pool when we see many short-lived preflights from a single key across many IPs. Immediate mitigation options: 1) stop preflight-per-tab, 2) cache preflight in-process for ~30-60s, 3) splay warmup, 4) reuse session-scoped ephemeral tokens.

lucy_cs: They asked for a short sample we can paste in their repo.

ryan_oncall: Share this minimal pattern (Node):
```
// per-process cached preflight + jitter
let pf = {ts:0,res:null};
async function preflightCached(){
  if (Date.now()-pf.ts < 45000) return pf.res;
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, Math.floor(Math.random()*300)+50));
  pf = {ts:Date.now(), res: await rw.preflight()};
  return pf.res;
}
```
And ensure the client uses one ephemeral token per session instead of requesting a fresh token per request. Also enable http2 keepalive and connection pooling.

dev-bot: Alert: key=rw_live_**** triggered onboarding-throttle at 2026-04-05T10:02:11Z. Window=90s, peak_rps=64, avg_rps=16. Recommendation: reduce concurrent preflight to <=20 or request temp warmup quota.

lucy_cs: Thanks. I've sent the snippet and asked them to drop concurrency to 20 for the next ramp test. They want to know: if they patch to a 30s cache and splay, should we still expect any 429s?

tom_infra: You might still see a small tail of 429s during the instant of rollout if many clients hit simultaneously, but caching + splay usually collapses the curve and drops 429s to <5% in our experience. If they need a clean warmup at >50 concurrency we can open a short ticket to temporarily raise per-key burst for 10 minutes.

ryan_oncall: Also tell them to log token_age in ms and include source ip in diagnostics so we can correlate. If they post the canary timestamp we'll lift a minor throttle for 10m while they ramp.

lucy_cs: Will do. They patched to 45s cache + 100-300ms splay and reduced parallelism to 18. Running a canary now. I'll post results and canary time here.

dev-bot: Note: If customers repeatedly request high parallelism for onboarding we recommend a Dedicated warmup plan. See internal playbook: https://wiki.redwood.ai/onboarding/warmups
